    {
      "id": "nx79x1c",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "EMERGENCY PROVISIONS"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Emergency provisions in India are incorporated in Part XVIII of the Constitution, spanning Articles 352 to 360.",
        "National Emergency (Article 352) can be declared on the grounds of war, external aggression, or armed rebellion.",
        "State Emergency or President's Rule (Article 356) is declared upon the failure of constitutional machinery in a state.",
        "Financial Emergency (Article 360) empowers the Centre to issue directions to states to observe canons of financial propriety.",
        "The 44th Amendment Act of 1978 introduced significant safeguards to prevent the misuse of emergency powers."
      ],
      "id": "xiodk0e"
    },
